<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The Windows shell, whole. Merged from Shenora.WinForms + Shenora.WebView2 +
    Shenora.WebView2.Sessions (owner, 2026-08-02) because the split protected a consumer that cannot
    exist: this kit's premise is React in a webview, so there is no Windows path that takes the
    WinForms primitives and not WebView2. The seam it preserved — WinForms with no IPC dependency —
    described an adoption STAGE, not a shipping configuration.

    The measurement that settled the last objection: Sessions adds NO dependency of its own (it
    already shared Microsoft.Web.WebView2), so folding it in costs ~28 KB of IL and no graph change.
    The "52.6 MB" figure raised against merging was dev-time RESTORE size, not shipped bytes — the
    WebView2 runtime is an Evergreen system component and what lands in an app's output is a small
    loader.

    Folders keep the three areas legible, which is the part of the old split worth keeping:
      Shell/      Windows primitives — bootstrap, frameless chrome, tray, secondary windows,
                  window state, STA dialogs/clipboard/shell, the UI-thread dispatcher
      WebView/    WebView2 hosting, serving, the IPC bridge, drop zones, window commands
      Sessions/   auxiliary browser sessions — render pool, interactive, streaming
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      TWO TFMs, and the second exists for exactly one capability. Everything in this shell compiles on plain
      net10.0-windows EXCEPT the system media transport: SystemMediaTransportControls is WinRT, and the WinRT
      projections only exist when the TFM names a Windows SDK version (measured: with a bare -windows,
      `Windows.Media` is not a namespace — CS0234).

      So existing consumers change NOTHING, and only an app that wants Now Playing retargets. The plain
      variant refuses BY NAME with the one-line fix in the message (WindowsPlaybackSession.Unsupported.cs)
      rather than being absent, so asking for it on the wrong TFM is a clear error and not a missing service.

      17763 (Windows 10 1809) is the LOWEST ref pack .NET offers, and nothing here needs more — MediaPlayer
      and SystemMediaTransportControls date to Windows 10 1507. Pick the lowest that compiles, not the newest
      installed.

      ⚠ TargetFramework must be CLEARED, not merely left unset: src/Directory.Build.props sets it to net10.0
      for every src project, and when both it and TargetFrameworks are set the SINGULAR one wins — which
      builds a plain net10.0 variant and fails NETSDK1136 (WinForms needs a Windows platform). Met once.
    -->
    <TargetFramework />
    <TargetFrameworks>net10.0-windows;net10.0-windows10.0.17763.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <!--
      ⚠ THIS LOOKS REDUNDANT AND IS NOT. It currently restates the default — an unset
      SupportedOSPlatformVersion follows TargetPlatformVersion — so today it changes nothing. It is here as a
      LATCH, because that default is how the runtime floor gets raised by accident: bumping the TFM for one
      new API silently moves the minimum Windows every consumer needs, which is a far bigger change than the
      one being made. This package shipped exactly that defect for one commit (TFM 19041 with no pin ⇒
      TargetPlatformMinVersion 10.0.19041.0, i.e. "requires Windows 10 2004", nobody's decision).

      With the pin, raising the TFM leaves the floor here and CA1416 — a build ERROR in this repo — forces
      any newer API to be guarded or the floor to be raised DELIBERATELY. Keep them in step only on purpose.
    -->
    <!--
      Only on the VERSIONED TFM: the plain one has TargetPlatformVersion 7.0, and a SupportedOSPlatformVersion
      above it is NETSDK1135. Matched on "-windows10." rather than the exact TFM string ON PURPOSE — an exact
      match would stop applying the moment someone bumps the version, silently re-opening the floor-raise this
      latch exists to prevent.
    -->
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion Condition="$(TargetFramework.Contains('-windows10.'))">10.0.17763.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
    <UseWindowsForms>true</UseWindowsForms>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Shenora.Windows</PackageId>
    <!-- The WebView2 package always references its WPF assembly, whose WindowsBase differs from a
         WinForms-only project's — MSB3277 fires but the WPF dll is never loaded here. Demote it. -->
    <MSBuildWarningsAsMessages>$(MSBuildWarningsAsMessages);MSB3277</MSBuildWarningsAsMessages>
    <!--
      MECHANISM vocabulary only (D22). The Sessions half of this description used to read "login
      windows with per-provider persistent profiles" and "co-browse streaming primitives" — the exact
      scenario names P5.5 H9.7/H9.8 removed from the TYPES (LoginWindow -> InteractiveSession,
      CoBrowseSession -> StreamingSession). It survived because D22's audit method is "sweep the API
      baselines for domain words", and a csproj Description is not in any baseline — while being the
      single most public place that vocabulary appears (the nuget.org listing). Name what the types
      do, not one product they enable.
    -->
    <Description>Shenora's Windows shell: application bootstrapper with global exception handling, DPI-correct window-state persistence, single-instance guard with activate broadcast, secondary windows on their own STA threads, tray icon, frameless-chrome form, and the Windows implementations of the Shenora shell contracts (STA file dialogs, clipboard, shell launching, UI-thread dispatcher). Hosts WebView2: environment prewarm, dev-server vs packaged-frontend loading, navigation/new-window/download/permission/process-failure handling, drag-drop zones, and the postMessage IPC bridge with batched event push. Includes auxiliary browser sessions — off-screen driveable sessions with a bounded pool, interactive sessions over an isolated persistent profile, and streaming sessions (CDP screencast out, synthetic input in). Mechanisms only: the kit ships no workflow.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Shenora\Shenora.csproj" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Web.WebView2" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
